title: 17 July 1974, The London meeting
new-page: true
view-if: {! return AnatolianRules.cyprusAtilla1.briefingScene(Q) === "cyprus_briefing_17"; !}

= 17 July 1974, The London meeting

The approach to Britain had brought Ecevit to London for talks with Prime Minister Harold Wilson and Foreign Secretary James Callaghan. He came seeking more than an expression of concern: Turkey wanted Britain to participate in a joint intervention, or allow Turkish forces to operate through its bases. The meeting would determine whether Ankara could secure British cooperation before deciding its next move.

Ecevit made a fairly long speech. He said that if Sampson became established, no organization or country would be able to bring about a change in Cyprus, whereas such a situation would be unacceptable to Turkey and to the Turkish community there.

Wilson listened attentively. Ecevit made it clear that Turkey could not remain a spectator to the coup and put forward his proposal:

Ecevit: "If you do not want bloodshed and an irreparable wound in NATO, let us conduct the operation together. Agree to allow Turkish forces to use the British bases. If we enter the island from your bases, it will be bloodless. I am calling on you to fulfil your duty under the Treaty of Guarantee."

Wilson said they knew what sort of man Sampson was and shared their concerns:

Wilson: "The opportunity to restore the old order has not yet been lost."

Ecevit: "Every passing hour makes matters a little more difficult. Sampson must be removed and a new order established in Cyprus. The balance has been completely upset. We are determined to secure the Turkish community's safety. For that, the Turks must be given an outlet to the sea..."

Wilson: "Let us invite Greece too and hold a tripartite meeting here. Kissinger is continuing his efforts as well. The United Nations is meeting. There is no need for military action."

Ecevit: "The balance in Cyprus has been upset beyond restoration. This cannot be put right by platonic statements or resolutions. Greece is responsible for the coup and cannot be accepted as a party."

The talks continued. Wilson wanted to steer matters towards negotiations:

Wilson: "We are in contact with Greece. We have asked for the replacement of the officers responsible for the coup and are awaiting a reply."

Ecevit was conciliatory and made no threats. As a diplomat at the meeting put it, he did not use grand declarations such as "if you do not do this, I shall go", as had happened in the past.

Ecevit: "We must think humanely. The present Sampson regime will change. If Turkey is forced to act, it will have to do so alone. Blood will be shed, whereas we could settle this by creating as little conflict as possible. The only way to do that is to use your bases."

This time Callaghan interjected:

Callaghan: "There will be war with Greece. Athens cannot stand by and watch. NATO will be shaken."

Having tried every avenue without obtaining a result, the British prime minister ended the talks:

Wilson: "The status of the bases has been defined quite clearly... There is no possibility of sending Turkish troops out from them... Nor do we accept the view that the Treaty of Guarantee gives Britain the right to armed intervention."

